<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="be4f95c3">&lt;p&gt;
    In any project, change is inevitable whether it comes from within the project or from external sources, therefore it
    should have an organizational structure to identify, assess and control any potential and approved
    changes to what was originally agreed for the project.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Once the project plan and other key associated documents have been approved, these become the project &quot;baselines&quot; and
    can only be changed after approval by the appropriate authority. Change control structures are not there to
    prevent changes, but to verify that every change is agreed by the relevant authority before implementation.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="externalId" nodeId="d1f8562a"></attribute>
<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="bdda959c"></attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="584f9743"></attribute>
<attribute name="purpose" nodeId="6b6541fa">To provide project level change governance procedures and define responsibilities for project change control.</attribute>
<attribute name="impactOfNotHaving" nodeId="3e6ad78d"></attribute>
<attribute name="reasonsForNotNeeding" nodeId="14d9eb04"></attribute>
<attribute name="briefOutline" nodeId="3596a865">&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Areas of responsibility and level of authority 
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Level of responsiveness
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Team members (including delegates if any)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Meeting approach (schedule, type, etc.)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Reporting requirements
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Positioning (interlocks with other groups, areas of joint ownership, escalation paths)&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
